Notebook[{
Cell["\<\
Lecture Two,
15 January 2003
DEFINING FUNCTIONS\
\>", "Title"],

Cell["Simple One-Line Function Definition", "Subtitle"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(f[x_]\  := \ x^2\)], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
The above line defines a function that takes the square of its argument.   x_ \
(a symbol  x with an underscore) is called a pattern (which \"match\" \
anything), and := is called delayed assignment, different from assignment =.  \
We use the function like this (without the underscore):\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(f[2]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(4\)], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(f[a]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(a\^2\)], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(f[1 + x]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\((1 + x)\)\^2\)], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell["This function takes any argument, including strings:", "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(f["\<this\>"]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \("this"\^2\)], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell["Assignment = and Delayed Assignment :=", "Subtitle"],

Cell["\<\
= (or Set[ ])  : the right-hand side expression is evaluated, and result sets \
to the variable (expression) on the left-hand side.
:= (or SetDelayed[ ]) : the right-hand side expression without evaluation is \
set to the variable (expression) on the left-hand side.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(a\  = \ 3; \ x\  = \ a + 1\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(4\)], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(?x\)\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \("Global`x"\)], "Print"],

Cell[BoxData[
    InterpretationBox[
      StyleBox[\(x\  = \ 4\),
        ShowStringCharacters->True,
        NumberMarks->True],
      InputForm[ 
        Definition[ x]],
      Editable->True,
      AutoDelete->True]], "Print"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell["\<\
With Set command \"=\", we associate number 4 with the variable x, since a + \
1 has been evaluated to 4 before set the value for x.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(a\  = \ 3; \ x\  := \ a\  + \ 1\)], "Input"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(?x\)\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \("Global`x"\)], "Print"],

Cell[BoxData[
    InterpretationBox[
      StyleBox[\(x\  := \ a\  + \ 1\),
        ShowStringCharacters->True,
        NumberMarks->True],
      InputForm[ 
        Definition[ x]],
      Editable->True,
      AutoDelete->True]], "Print"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell["\<\
With SetDelayed :=, we associated a + 1 with x, the expression a+1 is delayed \
for evaluation. \
\>", "Text"],

Cell["How to see all the variables defined/used by the user?", "Text"],

Cell["Clear or Remove a Function or Variable", "Subtitle"],

Cell["\<\
When we say Clear[x], the value associated with x is cleared, but the \
variable x still exists; to remove x completely from the memory, use \
Remove[x].   Clear or remove symbol when it is no longer needed.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell["More on Function Definition", "Subtitle"],

Cell["\<\
If we like to have a function that takes only integer, we can say:\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(g[x_Integer]\  := \ x^2\)], "Input"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \({g[3], \ g[x], \ g[3.5]}\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    RowBox[{"{", 
      RowBox[{"9", ",", \(g[x]\), ",", 
        RowBox[{"g", "[", 
          StyleBox["3.5`",
            StyleBoxAutoDelete->True,
            PrintPrecision->2], "]"}]}], "}"}]], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "If an expression such as g[x] or g[3.5] is given to ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " for which ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " does not known how to evaluate, it is returned without evaluation."
}], "Text"],

Cell["We can define a function with any number of arguments:", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(h[a_, \ b_, \ c_]\  := \ \((a + b + c)\)\)], "Input"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(h[1, 2, q]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(3 + q\)], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell["We can define function with a variable number of arguments:", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(v[a__]\  := \ Plus[a]\)], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
The Plus[ ] function adds up its arguments.  In fact, Plus[ ] is the internal \
form of  addition +;  e.g.,  a + b is identical to Plus[a,b]. \
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \({v[1], \ v[1, 3], \ v[1, 2, 3, 4]}\)], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
Two underscores match one or more arguments, three underscores match zero or \
more auguments.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell["\<\
Function Definition with More than One-Line of Code: Programs\
\>", "Subtitle"],

Cell["\<\
If we define a function with slightly complicated code of more than one line, \
we can use the Module[ ] to group the code as a single unit.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(\(prog[
        x_]\)\(\ \)\(:=\)\(\ \)\(Module[\ {a, b, c}, \ \n\t\t\ta\  = \ 
          1; \n\t\t\tb\  = \ 2; \n\t\t\tc\  = \ a\  + \ b; \n\t\t\tx\  + \ 
          c]\)\(\t\)\)\)], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
Note: the variables in the first list {a,b,c} specify LOCAL variables to this \
function. Statements are separated by semicolon \";\".   The return value of \
the function is last expression, x + c, without an ending semicolon.  The \
argument x must be treated as a constant, rather than a variable (i.e., you \
cannot do, e.g., x = a + b).\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(prog[3]\)], "Input"],

Cell["Parentheses/Brackets in Mathematica", "Subtitle"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " distinguishes carefully various form of brackets for syntactic purpose."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\((\ \ \ )\)\ \ \ \ \ \ Round\ parenthesis\ for\ grouping\), "\
\[IndentingNewLine]", 
    \(\([\ \ ]\)\ \ \ \ \ \ \ Square\ bracket\ for\ arguments\), "\
\[IndentingNewLine]", 
    \({\ \ }\ \ \ \ \ \ \ Braces\ for\ List\), "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
    \(\([\([\ ]\)]\)\ \ \ Double\ bracket\ for\ indexing\)}], "Input"],

Cell["Semi-Colon vs. Coma", "Subtitle"],

Cell["\<\
Semi-colon  \";\" separates multiple expressions.  You can put any number of \
them in your code.

Coma \",\" separates argument list.   The number of comas are dictated by the \
syntax requirement.  You can not put them arbitrarily.  Examples:\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(x = 3; \ y\  = \ x^2; \ z\  = \ x + 1\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(func[x_]\  := \ Module[{}, \ x^2]\)], "Input"],

Cell["Patterns", "Subtitle"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Pattern is an important concept in ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "'s syntax:  f[x_] is a pattern.  Patterns are used for matching an \
expression.  So f[x_] matched any expression with an explicit f, followed by \
square brackets with x_ being replaced by any expression.  So f[x_] matches \
f[1], f[x], or f[\"this\"], but not g[2].   If pattern matches, the \
definition associated with that pattern is executed.   In the above example, \
f[something] is replaced by something^2.  \n\ng[x_Integer]  matches a \
function g with integer argument, so, it matches g[2], but not g[a] (assuming \
we did not say a = 3, so it is not an integer).  Unmatched patterns are not \
executed and returned the same as input.   h[a_, b_, c_] matches function h \
with three arbitrary arguments.\n\nv[a__] (two underscores) matches function \
v with 1 or more arguments.  So it matches v[1], v[1,2],  or v[a,b,c].\n\nYou \
can test if pattern pattn match expression expr by the command:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(MatchQ[expr, \ pattn]\)], "Input"],

Cell["For example,", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(MatchQ[f[y], \ f[x_]]\)], "Input"],

Cell["Here are some more examples of patterns:", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(f[n_]\ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ f\ with\ any\ argument\ named\ n\), "\
\[IndentingNewLine]", 
    \(f[n_, m_]\ \ f\ with\ two\ argument\), "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
    \(f[n_, n_]\ \ two\ \ idential\ argument\), "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
    \(x^n_\ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ x\ to\ any\ power\), \
"\[IndentingNewLine]", 
    \(x_^n_\ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ any\ expr\ to\ any\ power\), "\
\[IndentingNewLine]", 
    \(a_\  + \ 
      b_\ \ \ \ \ \ \ a\ sum\ of\ two\ expressions\), "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
    \({a1_, \ a2_}\ \ a\ list\ of\ two\ exprs\)}], "Input"],

Cell["More on Patterns  _:  _?  _/;", "Subtitle"],

Cell["\<\
The function j[ ] has optional arguments with default values 1 and 2.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(j[x_, \ y_:  1, z_:  2]\  = \ jp[x, y, z]\)], "Input"],

Cell["The function below accepts only prime numbers as input:", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(p[n_?PrimeQ]\  := \ n^2\)], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
Similarly, the following function is defined only if the input satisfies a \
specified condition:\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(h[n_Integer\  /; \ n > \ 2]\  := \ n^3\)], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
We can also specify the condition at the end of the definition:\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(h2[n_Integer]\  := \ n^3\ \ \  /; \ n\  > \ 2\)], "Input"],

Cell["Multiple Definitions to the Same Symbol", "Subtitle"],

Cell["\<\
We can associate several definitions, differentiated by different pattern \
matchings. [Similar to the concept of polymorphism and function overloading \
in C++.]\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(Remove[f];\)\), "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
    \(\(f[n_Integer]\  := \ n;\)\), "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
    \(\(f[x_Real]\  := \ Floor[x];\)\), "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
    \(\(f[c_Complex]\  := \ Re[c];\)\), "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
    \(\(f[x_, \ n_Integer]\  := \ x^n;\)\)}], "Input"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(?f\)\)], "Input"],

Cell["Global`f", "Print",
  CellTags->"Info3251552865-2002582"],

Cell[BoxData[
    InterpretationBox[GridBox[{
          {GridBox[{
                {\(f[n_Integer] := n\)},
                {" "},
                {\(f[x_Real] := Floor[x]\)},
                {" "},
                {\(f[c_Complex] := Re[c]\)},
                {" "},
                {\(f[x_, n_Integer] := x\^n\)}
                },
              GridBaseline->{Baseline, {1, 1}},
              ColumnWidths->0.999,
              ColumnAlignments->{Left}]}
          },
        GridBaseline->{Baseline, {1, 1}},
        ColumnAlignments->{Left}],
      Definition[ "f"],
      Editable->False]], "Print",
  CellTags->"Info3251552865-2002582"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "When there are multiple definitions associated with a symbol, the first \
one matching the input will be used.  [",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " tries to apply the more specific one first before apply more general \
one.] "
}], "Text"],

Cell["Substitution Operations", "Subtitle"],

Cell["\<\
A convenient way for evaluating an expression is by variable substitution. \
\>", "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\((1 + x)\)^5\ \ \  /. \ x -> \ 3\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(1024\)], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell["\<\
While x = 3 globally sets x to 3, x -> 3 replaces x by 3 only locally at the \
current expression.  The symbol x itself is untouched.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell["\<\
The combined operator -> (minus sign followed by arrow without a space) is \
known as replacement Rule.   x->3 is the same as Rule[x,3].    /.  (slash \
followed by a period) is substitution operator.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \({Sin[x], Cos[x], Exp[x]} /. x -> 0\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \({0, 1, 1}\)], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Solve[x^2\  + \ 3  x\  - 1\  == \ 0, x]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \({{x \[Rule] 1\/2\ \((\(-3\) - \@13)\)}, {x \[Rule] 
          1\/2\ \((\(-3\) + \@13)\)}}\)], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(x\  /. \ %19\  // \ N\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \({\(-3.30277563773199478`\), 0.302775637731994562`}\)], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell["\<\
Expression of the form x -> 1.2 is called a rule, and output from solving an \
equation gives you a list of rules.  \
\>", "Text"],

Cell["\<\
What is the difference between { x -> 1, y -> 2}  vs { {x->1},{x->2}}?\
\>", "Text"],

Cell["\<\
Prefix, Postfix, and Infix Notation 
for Function Application.\
\>", "Subtitle"],

Cell["We normally say Sin[x], but we could say:", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Sin\ @\ Pi\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Pi\  // \ Sin\)], "Input"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \({a, b, c}\ ~Join~\ {d, f}\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \({a, b, c, d, f}\)], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell["The *fixes", "Subsubtitle"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(f[x]\ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ Apply\ function\ \ usual\ form\), "\
\[IndentingNewLine]", 
    \(f\ @\ x\ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ Prefix\ form\), "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
    \(x\  // \ f\ \ \ \ \ Postfix\ form\), "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
    \(a\ ~f~\ 
        b\ \ \ Infix\ form\ \((if\ f\ takes\ two\ arguments)\)\)}], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Clear[x, y]\)], "Input"],

Cell["Precedence of @ vs //", "Subtitle"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(f\ @\ x\  + \ y\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(x\  + \ y\  // \ f\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(x\ ~f~\ y\)], "Input"],

Cell["\<\

READINGS:\
\>", "Title"],

Cell["\<\
On function definitions:  Sec. 1.7
On patterns: Sec. 2.3\
\>", "Subtitle"]
},
FrontEndVersion->"4.1 for Microsoft Windows",
ScreenRectangle->{{0, 1024}, {0, 695}},
